Rollbacks in GitOps using Git History

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring how rollbacks work in GitOps using Git history 🚀
This is one of the biggest advantages of GitOps — making deployments safe and easily reversible.

🔹 Safe Deployments
 In GitOps, every change goes through:
Git commits
Pull requests
Version tracking
This ensures that all deployments are controlled and auditable, reducing the risk of failures in production.

🔹 Version Rollback using Git
 If a deployment causes issues:
Simply revert to a previous stable commit in Git
GitOps tools automatically detect the change
The system is restored to the previous working state
No need for manual fixes or complex rollback scripts.

🔹 Why It Matters in Real Projects
Faster recovery from failures
Reduced downtime
Reliable release management
Confidence in deployments

💡 Key Insight:
 GitOps turns Git into a powerful rollback mechanism, making deployments safer and easier to manage in production environments.
